module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_guard_in_emitted_shell_built_on_an_unquoted_expansion

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { NoRungStated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

// A GUARD THAT READS RIGHT AND TESTS WRONG. The bash grammar's word kinds each carry their own
// emitted spelling: a literal comes out single-quoted, a word variable comes out double-quoted
// ("$NAME"), and a command substitution comes out bare, $(...). That last one is the correct
// spelling of the kind -- it is not a defect in the serializer -- but a producer that reaches for
// the substitution kind where a guard needs an argument gets shell that splits and glob-expands the
// value before the predicate ever sees it. `[ -z $(git status --porcelain) ]` is the instance: with
// a clean worktree it is `[ -z ]`, which is true because the bare `-z` is a non-empty string; with
// one changed path it is `[ -z M ]`, false; with two or more it is `[ -z M a M b ]`, which is not a
// result at all -- the builtin writes "[: too many arguments" and exits 2. The step still lands on
// the branch its author wanted (the error makes the `&&` chain false, so the guarded work is
// skipped), which is exactly why this class survives review: the behavior is right, the diagnostic
// is a lie, and a path containing `*`, `?` or a space can glob or split before the test sees it.
//
// HOW IT HIDES. It hides in generated text, so nobody reads it: the artifact is the output of a
// producer, and the review surface is the producer, where the node looks fine -- a test bracket with
// two words, one of them a substitution. The claim that catches it has to read the EMITTED string,
// and it has to assert the negative too, or an added variable next to the surviving bare
// substitution would satisfy a positive-only check.
//
// HOW TO SPOT IT. Read the emitted word, not the node: in generated shell, look for a test bracket
// whose argument is `$(`, or for any expansion sitting where a word could split. Then ask which word
// kind the producer used and what that kind's emitted spelling is -- the grammar's quoting is
// per-kind, so the question "did this producer get a quoted argument?" is answered by the kind, not
// by the producer's intent.
//
// WHAT ENDS IT. A producer that captures the expansion into a variable and tests the quoted
// variable, which is the shape the grammar already renders correctly; or a grammar that offers a
// quoted-substitution word kind, which it does not have today. The first is local and was taken;
// the second is a grammar decision, and DESIGN section 6b's warning applies to whoever takes it:
// quoting the substitution kind changes every command substitution in every generated artifact,
// including the ones in assignment position where quoting is a no-op and the ones, if any, that
// depend on splitting.
data a_guard_in_emitted_shell_built_on_an_unquoted_expansion: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_guard_in_emitted_shell_built_on_an_unquoted_expansion" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: NoRungStated },

  receipts: [
    "generated shell whose predicate argument is an unquoted expansion: the value is word-split and glob-expanded before the test builtin receives it, so the builtin can error instead of answering, and the step reaches its branch through an exit status that misdescribes the decision",
    "it hides because the review surface is the producer, where the node reads fine -- a bracket test with two words, one a substitution -- and the artifact that carries the wrong shell is generated, so nobody reads it",
    "spot it on the emitted text, not the node: a test bracket whose argument begins with $( in generated shell, or any expansion in a position where a word could split; then ask which word kind the producer used, because the grammar quotes per kind",
    "ends when a producer captures the expansion into a variable and tests the quoted variable -- the shape the grammar renders correctly today -- or when the grammar offers a quoted-substitution kind, which is a corpus-wide emission change and needs its own acceptance pass",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.repo.repo_self_build", decl_name: "tracked_tree_identity_stmt"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.repo.repo_self_build", decl_name: "tracked_tree_dirty_assign_stmt"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.seed_build_preamble_no_marker_witness", decl_name: "the_identity_guard_tests_a_quoted_variable_not_a_bare_substitution"),
  ],
}
